WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.755
Hi, and welcome to our webcast today on
MBA student insights, women at the GSB.

00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:10.423
My name is Toda Allouez, and I'm a first
year MBA student here at the GSB.

00:00:10.423 --> 00:00:14.918
And today I'm joined by Rishi Lilly and
Val Young, who are both second years and

00:00:14.918 --> 00:00:16.071
about to graduate.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.730
We'll be talking about the student
experience here on campus.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.530
And we're here to answer as many
questions as you might have.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:27.100
To submit a question, just click on
the chat button at the top of your screen,

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.250
put in the question, and
click send to all the panelists.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.811
So today we won't be talking about
anything related to admissions process,

00:00:33.811 --> 00:00:37.368
or the application, or financial aid,
but people from the staff from the MBA

00:00:37.368 --> 00:00:39.910
admissions office are here
to answer your questions.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.928
You just type the question
into the chat box.

00:00:42.928 --> 00:00:45.181
So let's start with some introductions.

00:00:45.181 --> 00:00:48.379
Rishi and Val, please just tell
us a little bit about yourselves,

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:49.407
where you're from,

00:00:49.407 --> 00:00:53.192
what you did before the GSB, and what
you're looking to do after graduation.

00:00:53.192 --> 00:00:54.840
So-
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.721
&gt;&gt; Yes Rishi you can go first, yeah.

00:00:56.721 --> 00:01:01.897
&gt;&gt; I'm from Texas, born and raised, went
to University of Chicago for undergrad.

00:01:01.897 --> 00:01:05.307
And I've spent four years in
Chicago doing executive search,

00:01:05.307 --> 00:01:09.049
where I helped build additional
health practice and then came here.

00:01:09.049 --> 00:01:11.231
And after school,
I'll be doing health tech.

00:01:11.231 --> 00:01:13.275
&gt;&gt; Nice, thank you Rishi.

00:01:13.275 --> 00:01:14.714
You want to go now, Val?

00:01:14.714 --> 00:01:15.457
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:01:15.457 --> 00:01:19.405
Hi, I'm Val and
I am from New Hampshire originally.

00:01:19.405 --> 00:01:24.002
Went to Bowdoin College in Brunswick,
Maine, I graduated in 2008.

00:01:24.002 --> 00:01:28.794
Shortly thereafter, I moved to DC and
I spent about six years after

00:01:28.794 --> 00:01:32.826
college before coming to
business school in politics.

00:01:32.826 --> 00:01:36.355
Most recently I was in
the US Senate as a Chief of Staff.

00:01:36.355 --> 00:01:40.948
Over the summer I worked at the Boston
Consulting Group in San Francisco, so

00:01:40.948 --> 00:01:43.693
I'll be going back there after graduation.

00:01:43.693 --> 00:01:49.060
I am interested in hopefully starting
something on my own at some point.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.790
But spending a little time in consulting

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:56.300
before figuring out exactly
what my career path will be.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.430
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Val.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.580
So for me, again, I'm Toda Allouez.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.750
I'm originally from Nigeria, and

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:04.290
I moved to the US about
seven years ago for college.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.838
I went to Grinnell College in Iowa.

00:02:06.838 --> 00:02:08.530
I double majored in computer science and
math,

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:11.170
and after that I worked as
a programmer at Goldman.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:16.980
And then I did that for two years and
then came to the GSB last September.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.580
After the GSB, I'm looking to remain in
technology, but do something more like

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.150
business development related
versus being a programmer or

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:25.660
software engineer on the tech side.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:31.283
So let's get started with
questions from you guys.

00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:35.609
So the first question is,
what's it like for women at Stanford,

00:02:35.609 --> 00:02:38.295
both inside and outside the classroom?

00:02:38.295 --> 00:02:38.992
&gt;&gt; You want to start?

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:40.431
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:02:40.431 --> 00:02:44.736
So I think I was pleasantly
surprised actually by

00:02:44.736 --> 00:02:49.255
the sort of climate and
atmosphere for women here.

00:02:49.255 --> 00:02:54.069
Our class, at least the class of 2016,
I'm not sure about the class for

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:58.056
the next year, was about a 60/40 split,
male to female,

00:02:58.056 --> 00:03:02.796
and we had one of the highest percentages
of women at the GSB in history and so

00:03:02.796 --> 00:03:04.550
that's been really nice.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:11.030
I think that I have found a really
supportive group of male and

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:12.650
female friends here.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:16.640
I definitely have found there are some
challenges coming into a place that, or

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:22.085
into an industry sort of as whole,
that has been historically male dominated,

00:03:22.085 --> 00:03:25.795
and I think the GSB and the student
body are working through that and

00:03:25.795 --> 00:03:27.645
sort of making progress as we go along.

00:03:27.645 --> 00:03:29.585
And I'm happy to, obviously,
talk about that a bit more.

00:03:30.695 --> 00:03:31.575
But, by and large,

00:03:31.575 --> 00:03:37.225
I've actually found it much more inclusive
than I even anticipated coming in.

00:03:37.225 --> 00:03:39.348
So, that's been really nice.

00:03:39.348 --> 00:03:44.550
&gt;&gt; Okay, I think the thing that I'd add
onto that is I think starting our year,

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.477
we had this woman in management board, and

00:03:47.477 --> 00:03:52.010
we started having formal members
who were men, women, join.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:57.600
And we've had such a huge movement
of guys being involved in women.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.600
And you just feel the support for
what used to be considered women's

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.450
issues as just issues that we all
talk about, and we all think about.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.640
So there's definitely an open environment
to talk about what it means to be

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.820
a working mom, or to just like a working
parent rather than saying working mom.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:18.680
And conversations like that,
which is really great and refreshing.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:21.420
&gt;&gt; I actually was thinking
of one other thing that,

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:24.110
there's been some really great
formal stuff that Stanford has done.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:29.950
I think informally, one of the things
that our classmates have really pushed is

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:34.620
diversifying some of those kind of side
conversations and group meetings and

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.760
things, particularly about
entrepreneurship and the start up world.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.530
So I think we've found in the first
quarter, maybe first two quarters,

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:44.890
there were a lot of male groups
that were having informal and

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:48.870
formal conversations about
entrepreneurship and for

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:52.800
whatever reason women weren't
really as integrated into that.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.320
I think women were having their
own separate conversations.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.940
And I know a few classmates of ours made
a very concerted effort to start bridging

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:04.830
those two groups together, start thinking
about forming startup teams together.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:10.290
And just kind of allowing that ecosystem
to cross over between male students and

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:10.970
female students.

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:14.920
And that has been something that I've
really appreciated going forward,

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:19.020
because I think those are the informal
networks and informal groups that start to

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:22.920
really make a big difference as
you move through your career.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.013
&gt;&gt; Thank you, very good points.

00:05:25.013 --> 00:05:30.440
So the next question is, how do you think
about timing to get your MBA in terms of

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:36.135
stepping out of your career for two years
and possibly starting a family some day?

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:37.598
&gt;&gt; I can start with that one.

00:05:37.598 --> 00:05:42.498
I think getting our babies is a little
bit similar to having getting our MBA.

00:05:42.498 --> 00:05:44.680
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes, exactly.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:48.378
Getting our MBAs are little
similar to having a baby,

00:05:48.378 --> 00:05:51.019
which is there may be no perfect time.

00:05:51.019 --> 00:05:54.526
I think when one time when it is a really
good time is if you want to pivot, and

00:05:54.526 --> 00:05:56.600
especially with regard to having a family.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:59.760
I know a lot of families actually
have a baby while they're at the GSB,

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.350
maybe in the second year because that
seems like a good transition point.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:06.944
So I guess my advice would be to
figure out first is this something you

00:06:06.944 --> 00:06:07.709
really want?

00:06:07.709 --> 00:06:10.054
Is this something that's
going to benefit your career?

00:06:10.054 --> 00:06:12.376
And really think about how and why.

00:06:12.376 --> 00:06:15.982
And then just after that, if you want it,
and if you want a family too,

00:06:15.982 --> 00:06:19.667
just decide how you're going to
start piecing those things together.

00:06:19.667 --> 00:06:23.894
It will never be perfect, the timing
will never be great, but you can do it.

00:06:23.894 --> 00:06:30.354
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would say as somebody that got
married in my first month of business

00:06:30.354 --> 00:06:37.338
school, I would echo Rishi's thoughts
that the best laid plans always go awry.

00:06:37.338 --> 00:06:42.075
So I had worked for
about six years before I came, I think.

00:06:42.075 --> 00:06:45.346
Again to Rishi's point, it felt like
I had had some really awesome and

00:06:45.346 --> 00:06:49.520
interesting accomplishments and
I was ready for some new challenges.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:52.080
I think spending some time
thinking about when and

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.040
why you want to go is really important,
and I would sort of prioritize that.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:01.765
The family stuff I think is tough for
women at any point in our careers,

00:07:01.765 --> 00:07:05.410
at business school and
career trajectory thereafter.

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:10.680
And it's something that you and
your family and

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.770
your partner have to spend
some time thinking about.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:15.110
I think that business school,

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:17.850
it'll fit into your life however
you need it to fit into your life.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.325
If you're doing something like I'm doing,

00:07:21.325 --> 00:07:24.629
which is kind of going into
a consulting firm where you're going to

00:07:24.629 --> 00:07:28.604
have a couple of years where you may want
to not have kids at that point in time,

00:07:28.604 --> 00:07:32.359
you may want to think about applying
a year or two earlier than you might.

00:07:32.359 --> 00:07:36.670
I mean I'm 30, I'm probably slightly
on the older side of the GSB, or

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:40.908
average older, and it is something
we've thought about at one point,

00:07:40.908 --> 00:07:44.178
when will we graduate,
when do we want to have a family?

00:07:44.178 --> 00:07:48.367
So I think you make it work, but it's
something that you may want to at some

00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:53.182
point kind of look to the future and start
backing out and saying, what are my goals?

00:07:53.182 --> 00:07:56.718
Because I think your family goals
are as important as your career and

00:07:56.718 --> 00:07:57.959
your education goals,

00:07:57.959 --> 00:08:02.444
and you should just make sure that you're
thinking about those two things in tandem.

00:08:02.444 --> 00:08:07.432
But I will say I think that the GSB is
a place where you can accommodate whatever

00:08:07.432 --> 00:08:12.180
you choose and whatever needs
to be accommodated in your life.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:13.540
So, don't let it be a hindrance.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:17.160
But I would be thoughtful about it.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.460
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:22.830
Next question is, are there any unique
opportunities for women in the MBA program

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:29.220
who are interested in pursuing
a career in the nonprofit sector?

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:30.880
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:33.527
I did not pursue a career
in the nonprofit sector.

00:08:33.527 --> 00:08:35.055
I think you might have that.

00:08:35.055 --> 00:08:40.633
&gt;&gt; So I, when i came from the government
sector, which is different but

00:08:40.633 --> 00:08:46.580
related, there is the Social
Innovation Career Services Center.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:51.921
And I think that they have done a really
great job at building up a pretty robust,

00:08:51.921 --> 00:08:56.866
both curriculum for folks that
are interested in nonprofit management,

00:08:56.866 --> 00:09:01.411
sort of social innovation type fields,
and then they have a special

00:09:01.411 --> 00:09:05.583
certificate that you can sort
of apply your credits towards.

00:09:05.583 --> 00:09:08.173
And I think some really
great counselors and

00:09:08.173 --> 00:09:12.306
advisors that have had quite
a distinguished career in those fields.

00:09:12.306 --> 00:09:15.380
So I would say that I have kind of like,
dipped in and out of that a little bit.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:20.230
I will be getting the certificate, but I
haven't probably utilized it to the degree

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:25.690
that some of our classmates, that are very
focused on nonprofit sector have.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:30.318
That's at I think through 390s
which are our independent studies

00:09:30.318 --> 00:09:33.120
through the CSI program and a few others.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:37.718
There's a lot of opportunities to,
both within the curriculum and

00:09:37.718 --> 00:09:42.558
outside the curriculum, design,
sort of study, and internships and

00:09:42.558 --> 00:09:47.332
externships that allow you to get
involved in the nonprofit sectors.

00:09:47.332 --> 00:09:51.876
You may have to be a little bit more
creative if they're not TAC, or they're

00:09:51.876 --> 00:09:56.563
not San Francisco or Palo Alto affiliated
or located, but I think that there is

00:09:56.563 --> 00:10:01.080
a lot of support here, and a number of
resources to help you be successful.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:02.335
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thank you for that.

00:10:02.335 --> 00:10:05.145
Just one thing I would like to
add is actually about the social

00:10:05.145 --> 00:10:06.780
innovation trips.

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:07.600
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:12.060
we have social innovation trips,
like just like every other study trip, but

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:17.450
this trips are just concentrated and
focused on social innovation I guess.

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:21.333
Yeah, so I know we have a bunch
to countries in Africa.

00:10:21.333 --> 00:10:26.087
So we have one to Rwanda, Uganda,
maybe South Africa next year, and men,

00:10:26.087 --> 00:10:28.960
women included,
they all go on these trips.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:32.210
But these trips are a good example of how
you actually get to see the practical

00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:36.330
applications of different products and
different innovative ideas

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:40.030
on people's lives, and how it actually
impacts people in Rwanda, Uganda or Kenya.

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:44.120
So that's a very, very unique opportunity
that you can explore if you're interested.

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:46.610
&gt;&gt; I don't know if there are any
women specific opportunities.

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:50.560
I do know that Alena won an award, right?

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:51.900
For social innovation.

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:55.440
And a scholarship for the GSB.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:01.170
Also, Alena, I think and Ashley, right,
are starting their own nonprofit business.

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:03.130
So, there are women killing it-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:06.740
&gt;&gt; At the GSB in the nonprofit sector.

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:08.910
&gt;&gt; And there is, I don't want to
misspeak because I don't know enough

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:13.110
about the specifics, but there is
a good amount of matching funds and

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:17.970
fund raising available for students that
over the summer want to do nonprofit work

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:22.930
if they're working at a 501C3, and also
obviously really great loan forgiveness

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:25.990
programs for those that go into
non-profit work after the GSP.

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:29.892
So those are two things that
I'm sure the admissions and

00:11:29.892 --> 00:11:32.919
other staff members here could
talk about in more detail,

00:11:32.919 --> 00:11:36.826
but there is quite a bit of financial
assistance that's provided as well.

00:11:36.826 --> 00:11:38.580
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:41.430
So next question is how many of
the teachers and faculties are women?

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:44.010
Do you know a percentage?

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:46.360
&gt;&gt; I don't know the percentage,

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:50.500
I do know that some of our most
rockstar faculty members are women.

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:54.265
We have Maggie Neale for negotiation, I'm
actually doing independent study with her,

00:11:54.265 --> 00:11:56.835
she's an incredible human being,
fantastic researcher.

00:11:56.835 --> 00:12:01.020
We have Deb-
&gt;&gt; Grunfeld.

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:09.212
&gt;&gt; Yes exactly who with Amy Cutty
they did the work on Power Poses.

00:12:09.212 --> 00:12:11.849
Those are two of my favorite professors.

00:12:11.849 --> 00:12:12.760
Do you have?

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:14.380
&gt;&gt; Yeah I would say,

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:20.590
candidly,I think there are not
enough women faculty at the GSB.

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:25.090
My intuition tells me that,
that is probably true across

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:29.430
most of the business schools, certainly
most of the top business schools.

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:32.880
I think the GSB,
I know in their dean search and

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:37.030
in other communications that we've had
with the administration has really focused

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:42.120
on being particularly proactive
about increasing the numbers.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:44.610
Especially as we get up
to the ten year level.

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:48.960
It's not, I think, where many of us
would like it to be in a perfect world.

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:53.730
But there are some really fantastic women
and I think they get a lot of support.

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:56.525
Anne Beyer, who was my first
year accounting professor,

00:12:56.525 --> 00:12:59.720
won the Distinguished Teaching Award
from the students one year,

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:02.760
which is the student given award
to the best teacher at the GSB.

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:07.681
So, I don't have a number on percentage,
I think the percentage would be lower

00:13:07.681 --> 00:13:11.796
than what most of us in this room
would like it to be, I think that's

00:13:11.796 --> 00:13:17.170
a problem sort of across the board in
academia at this level and in this sector.

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:20.790
But it's something that we
talk about quite a bit,

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:24.840
the administration talks about quite a bit
and is trending in the right direction.

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:26.588
&gt;&gt; I do think women in
faculty are celebrated.

00:13:26.588 --> 00:13:28.874
&gt;&gt; Yes definitely, yeah for sure.

00:13:28.874 --> 00:13:31.720
I mean they're doing amazing things.

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:35.539
Some of them far outshine their male
colleagues and their male peers.

00:13:35.539 --> 00:13:38.970
But I think in a perfect world,
there'd be more of them, and

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:41.746
I think there will be more
of them as time goes on.

00:13:41.746 --> 00:13:42.890
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:45.887
Another faculty who's doing great work,
Carol Robin.

00:13:45.887 --> 00:13:47.510
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Carol is amazing, right.

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:48.070
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:49.380
&gt;&gt; Amazing, yeah.

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:51.380
Well, thank you for
the very honest answer.

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:54.640
Next question.

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:56.808
What has been your favorite
experience at a GSB?

00:13:59.708 --> 00:14:00.850
&gt;&gt; Mine is talk.

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:05.840
It's not a, I mean there's been
a million classroom things, and

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:07.980
a number of professors
that I'm super close with,

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:12.970
but my favorite thing about
the GSB is talk which we do,

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:17.970
the whole school and community is invited,
but mostly you do it with your class.

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:21.010
And it's kind of like Moth Radio Hour,
if anyone has ever heard of that.

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:26.720
It's basically 30 minutes or
so or an hour once a week

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:31.600
at night where everyone packs into
the MBA lounge and two people

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:36.740
give about a 30 minute talk of their life,
whatever they choose to focus on.

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:39.620
It's in those moments when
I'm at the GSB that I'm

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:43.720
just reminded of what a wonderful,
fantastic community we have.

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:48.560
And I feel most grateful for being there,
for getting to know these amazing

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:53.450
classmates, for hearing stories that
are so different and so, impactful.

00:14:53.450 --> 00:15:00.318
And I think that that's something
that will stick with me for decades.

00:15:00.318 --> 00:15:03.918
&gt;&gt; I want to add on to that and
then I'll share my favorite.

00:15:03.918 --> 00:15:08.577
I think another really special thing
about talk is how people are able to

00:15:08.577 --> 00:15:13.392
share things that they wouldn't usually
share in a different setting and

00:15:13.392 --> 00:15:16.887
in creating that,
not only a safe space for that, but

00:15:16.887 --> 00:15:20.949
also celebrating in a loving and
that bringing us all closer.

00:15:20.949 --> 00:15:24.879
It's a really wonderful and
I think unique thing that we have here and

00:15:24.879 --> 00:15:28.700
I'm sure other places have it as well,
but it's really special.

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:32.140
For my favorites,
I just love getting to know my classmates.

00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:35.551
And that sounds silly, probably.

00:15:35.551 --> 00:15:40.290
But as Val said, each person has
something incredible to share and

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:43.060
just warm, wonderful personalities.

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:45.780
There are very few people that I
wouldn't want to spend time with.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:48.250
And so, any of the activities around that.

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:52.154
So we have small group dinners where first
year you are in a lottery system and

00:15:52.154 --> 00:15:56.302
you get randomly assigned to, you can host
a small group generated with randomly

00:15:56.302 --> 00:15:59.789
assigned people, so you get to know
different people in the class.

00:15:59.789 --> 00:16:02.729
And that was one of my way to get
to know people whom I usually

00:16:02.729 --> 00:16:05.609
wouldn't run into and
I really loved that experience and

00:16:05.609 --> 00:16:09.399
with my really good friendships that
I came out of that So, we, thank you.

00:16:09.399 --> 00:16:11.118
&gt;&gt; Colley, you can say C4C.

00:16:11.118 --> 00:16:15.474
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [INAUDIBLE] She killed it,

00:16:15.474 --> 00:16:18.114
being the head of C4C.

00:16:18.114 --> 00:16:22.065
So C4C is Challenge for Charity,
no I actually really like C4C.

00:16:22.065 --> 00:16:24.580
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I really like it, I've been working so

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:26.620
hard to be on leadership for next year.

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:27.970
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:28.850
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And you

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:30.365
also like the CMC Committee, I know.

00:16:30.365 --> 00:16:32.795
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I like CMC.

00:16:32.795 --> 00:16:38.420
[LAUGH] But anyway, C4C is a sporting
event that happens, hosted by Stanford

00:16:38.420 --> 00:16:42.570
where west coast business schools are
invited to compete in different sports.

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:45.030
And Val was in charge of that,
this year was amazing.

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:48.070
I played basketball in college so
I brought my basketball skill.

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:49.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And I helped Stanford,

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:51.160
&gt;&gt; I did not play basketball in college,

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:53.009
but I also brought my basketball skills.

00:16:53.009 --> 00:16:53.805
&gt;&gt; Yes, and we killed it.

00:16:53.805 --> 00:16:55.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We killed it.

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:56.460
Yeah, it was really fun.

00:16:56.460 --> 00:17:01.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah it's the culmination of a year
long fundraising and volunteering.

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:03.650
Competition among nine west
coast business schools, and

00:17:03.650 --> 00:17:08.140
then we use the weekend to celebrate and
bring everyone together, so

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:12.790
there are 1300 students that come to
the GSB and it is a fantastic weekend.

00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:14.065
&gt;&gt; It is really fun.

00:17:14.065 --> 00:17:20.805
&gt;&gt; And with the dance competition,
and it is just amazing.

00:17:20.805 --> 00:17:22.640
Okay, next question.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:26.870
Has any particular club, including but
not limited to the women and

00:17:26.870 --> 00:17:31.180
business club, had a tangible impact on
your career in terms of giving you new

00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:35.620
opportunities that you might
not have known otherwise found?

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:40.220
&gt;&gt; So the clubs are great place to get new
opportunities, any of them, all of them.

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:45.020
And the way it works is a lot of jobs are
final thru and recruit management center,

00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:49.700
but the each club has specific connections
with people in their industry, and so

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:53.950
sometimes jobs go directly to
the club leaders and funnel that way.

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:56.700
So definitely if you want to be in
healthcare join the healthcare club.

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:59.520
If you want to be in VC join the VC club.

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:00.930
It's a great way to do it.

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:07.870
In general, our network is incredible,
and you should use it.

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:09.850
And I'm just talking
about your classmates.

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:15.320
So even more so than the club, if there's
a company that you really want to work at,

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:19.200
forget if they have a job open, reaching
out to a classmate who either works

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:22.680
there or knows someone who work there,
and can give you an intro actually

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:25.220
really helps in terms of,
especially if you want to pivot and

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:28.660
your resume wouldn't scream I
am the person for this job.

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:32.570
It's a really good way to get a foot
in there and to advocate for yourself.

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:36.100
So I would strongly recommend doing that,
not only at the GSB but in life in

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:40.180
general, figuring out how you can use
your network to get you to new places.

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:44.700
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that's true.

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:47.880
I would say, I'm a member of the VC Club,

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:51.460
though I don't have immediate
plans to go into Venture Capital.

00:18:51.460 --> 00:18:56.630
And there's been some amazing lunches and
dinners and opportunities to meet and

00:18:56.630 --> 00:19:00.770
network with people that I would not
probably have otherwise had if I had not

00:19:00.770 --> 00:19:05.350
been in the club or climbed onto
dinners that some of my friends host,

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:10.230
in the immediate term that's not something
that I'm going to probably capitalize on.

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:13.280
But long term these are some really cool,
fun,

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:17.920
interesting relationships and
interactions with people that I otherwise

00:19:17.920 --> 00:19:19.700
would never have been able
to make the connection with.

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:21.570
So.
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:25.255
I think we have a lot of questions, so
we will try to answer as many as possible.

00:19:25.255 --> 00:19:28.840
So the next one is, do you have any
plans to transition into a new industry.

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:36.040
A new role after your MBA and how do you
think that GSB has prepared you so far?

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:39.760
&gt;&gt; So
I have very strong opinions on this topic.

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:41.500
Yes and yes for me.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:44.270
I am currently still interviewing, but

00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:47.200
I said earlier I'm going to be working
health tech, either health tech or

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:50.330
tech and I'm in the process
of interviewing right now.

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:53.450
And how has the GSB prepared me for
the transition?

00:19:54.580 --> 00:19:57.710
First, I'm just going to stop here and
say, if you come to the GSB or

00:19:57.710 --> 00:20:02.370
anywhere and you know that you want to
pivot, do not take a breather on that.

00:20:02.370 --> 00:20:05.190
You need to figure out
what you want to do.

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:09.520
What steps will get you there and
start mapping that out immediately.

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:11.950
And this is something we talk
a lot about MBA two year.

00:20:11.950 --> 00:20:17.084
There's a lot of, do whatever you
want during the summer, [SOUND].

00:20:17.084 --> 00:20:20.158
And that can be wonderful, and
you may still want to do that and

00:20:20.158 --> 00:20:23.830
will not regret your decision but
you need to do it with full knowledge.

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:29.750
You can, if you want to make a huge pivot
using that summer to do it, is really key.

00:20:29.750 --> 00:20:32.210
It's much easier to get a summer
internship and a new function and

00:20:32.210 --> 00:20:38.650
a new role or either or or both,
than it is just to do it full time later.

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:41.160
So just think about that.

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:42.770
The next thing I'd say, what prepares you.

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:45.620
To both your classmates,

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:50.090
who can help you prepare for
interviews, which is extremely helpful.

00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:52.190
Since they've done the jobs before,
they've probably interviewed people for

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:56.100
those roles before,
it's really helpful to leverage that.

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:58.458
And on the other side,
some classes really help you.

00:20:58.458 --> 00:21:01.910
If you're going to go into a job where you
go and have to do financial modeling and

00:21:01.910 --> 00:21:03.030
you've never done that before.

00:21:03.030 --> 00:21:04.380
Take financial modeling.

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:08.240
You're going to go into operations,
take operations.

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:09.290
Make friends with the professor.

00:21:09.290 --> 00:21:10.850
Get to know more about the subject.

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:16.510
It's really upon you though, to know what
you want to get out of the experience and

00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:17.960
to be really focused about getting it.

00:21:19.280 --> 00:21:20.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would add,
I went into consulting,

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:23.570
which was totally different
from what I had done before.

00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:29.070
I used both the consulting club,
the group management center alumni,

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:34.710
a ton of GSB alumni that are in
different firms, to both learn about,

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:37.660
sort of show my interest in,
and prepare for interviews.

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:42.860
I think that, in particular,
our classmates that offered to

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:46.770
do case interviews with me was some of the
most meaningful and helpful that I got.

00:21:46.770 --> 00:21:51.270
So I think that has sort of facilitated,
in part, with career management center,

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:54.530
in part, separately through the consulting
club and just people giving their time.

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:58.880
So I think that that is true
across a number of industries, and

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:00.980
it's just my experience with consulting.

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:02.135
I would also say,

00:22:02.135 --> 00:22:07.785
I've been working through Startup Grads on
a startup with another classmate of mine.

00:22:07.785 --> 00:22:11.450
Startup Grad is just a really great class
if your interested in starting something

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:16.460
and that sort of design centered thinking
takes you through a couple of quarters

00:22:16.460 --> 00:22:18.930
really going from zero to six as startup.

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:22.120
And that has been really fantastic
as far as just introducing me to

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:27.570
entrepreneurship, connecting
me with investors and

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:31.230
leaders in the field and other students
that are doing something similar.

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:36.320
And so that, as a class, I think,
has been an unbelievable crash course

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:41.500
in entrepreneurship which is something
that long-term I hope to get into.

00:22:41.500 --> 00:22:42.480
And it's definitely been,

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:45.942
like kind of given me the training wheels
almost to go and do it on my own so.

00:22:45.942 --> 00:22:48.490
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:22:48.490 --> 00:22:52.820
The next question is, are there any
specific initiatives, whether in classes,

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:57.610
leadership coaching, or any of that,
that's helped prepare women students in

00:22:57.610 --> 00:23:03.580
particular for a career in traditionally
male dominated industries?

00:23:03.580 --> 00:23:04.330
&gt;&gt; Acting the power, right?

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:05.149
I mean [LAUGH], I-
&gt;&gt; Yeah

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:08.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a good, I mean, so

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:11.970
I think WIM focuses on
it quite a bit through

00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:18.220
different programming and
conversations that they facilitate.

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:22.200
I think that there are some really cool
classes, including the one we were talking

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:26.530
about with Deb Greenfield, which is
called, Acting with Power, which is all

00:23:26.530 --> 00:23:32.540
about understanding how you present,
how you take power, give away power.

00:23:32.540 --> 00:23:35.100
I think some of the things that we think
about when you think about women not

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:37.330
speaking up in meetings, or
how you sit in a meeting,

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:40.760
or how to approach
a difficult conversation.

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:45.470
I think-
&gt;&gt; There's women in entrepreneurship.

00:23:45.470 --> 00:23:47.110
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
That's been a great class actually.

00:23:47.110 --> 00:23:47.740
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:48.520
Which is also Vogus.

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:54.492
I don't know that there are a ton
of initiatives focussed on women

00:23:54.492 --> 00:23:57.620
getting into male dominated industries.

00:23:57.620 --> 00:24:02.547
There are male dominated industries and if
you're a woman who wants to get into that,

00:24:02.547 --> 00:24:07.063
all the support in the world to get you
there including male classmates who Were

00:24:07.063 --> 00:24:09.049
in or will be in those industries,

00:24:09.049 --> 00:24:12.883
professors who I know have helped
some of our female colleagues and

00:24:12.883 --> 00:24:16.965
classmates get into those industries
such as private equity search.

00:24:16.965 --> 00:24:18.860
[INAUDIBLE] So

00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:24.090
I think Women In Entrepreneurship is a
great example though of a specific class.

00:24:24.090 --> 00:24:28.600
So there's Professor Fern Mandelbaum
who's investing, angel investing,

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:30.820
venture capital a few other things.

00:24:30.820 --> 00:24:35.750
She hosts a class specifically
around sort of encouraging women to

00:24:35.750 --> 00:24:37.660
get into entrepreneurship
either by starting something or

00:24:37.660 --> 00:24:39.520
getting into the investing side.

00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:44.350
And brings in a ton of women in the valley
and also men in the valley that

00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:46.920
are really supportive of getting more
women involved in those industries.

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:50.720
And that is sort of soft skills
like meeting these people,

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:53.840
hearing from other people that
have a similar world view to you.

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:55.900
And being able to picture
yourself in that role and

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:59.160
also some really tangible
conversations that we

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:02.550
had amongst us about what's holding women
back from being in those industries.

00:25:02.550 --> 00:25:05.304
What would you look for, you know how
do we start to change the culture.

00:25:05.304 --> 00:25:10.839
So that's a great example,
there is also a lot of programming around

00:25:10.839 --> 00:25:16.481
women through the CMC and others
just about like public speaking and.

00:25:16.481 --> 00:25:19.244
You can get one on one coaching and
things like that.

00:25:19.244 --> 00:25:20.876
&gt;&gt; And negotiations.

00:25:20.876 --> 00:25:21.812
&gt;&gt; Negotiations, yeah.

00:25:21.812 --> 00:25:26.844
&gt;&gt; There's a lot of support,
yeah absolutely.

00:25:26.844 --> 00:25:29.139
&gt;&gt; Thank you, next question.

00:25:29.139 --> 00:25:32.362
Do you have any insights on
what the experience is like for

00:25:32.362 --> 00:25:35.734
partners joining their girlfriends or
wives at Stanford?

00:25:35.734 --> 00:25:39.149
Especially given the location of Stanford
and the living experience here on campus.

00:25:40.850 --> 00:25:45.700
I have a fantastic GSP SO,
significant other,

00:25:45.700 --> 00:25:49.480
husband who lives on campus with me so
I live in Escondido Village.

00:25:49.480 --> 00:25:54.570
I have this awesome one bedroom which
is so below market rate in Palo Alto.

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:55.540
It's incredible.

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:59.720
If I could squat there for
the next ten years, I would stay.

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:01.280
And I think so my husband.

00:26:01.280 --> 00:26:05.150
It is definitely different having a male,
significant other like a male SO

00:26:05.150 --> 00:26:08.800
than what is more traditional
which is females SO's probably.

00:26:08.800 --> 00:26:13.180
My husband loves Palo Alto,
which really helps.

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:15.770
He says it's like our country club
that he gets to come home to.

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:20.400
He works in the city, he has made a great
network of both other male SOs and

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:21.930
then some of my male classmates.

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:25.980
It definitely took a little bit of time
for him to get himself into the community.

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:29.445
He takes the CalTrain into San Francisco.

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:34.920
It's not a perfect commute,
but tons of people do it.

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:36.780
He had like a really good support network.

00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:39.930
I think that the best thing for him which
has been so great, is he has been able to

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:45.040
tap into the GSB network for
his own career ideas and transition.

00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:49.090
So he recently moved from energy
clean tech to health tech,

00:26:49.090 --> 00:26:51.280
and really utilized the GSP network for
that.

00:26:51.280 --> 00:26:55.380
Both the SO community and the student
community, and I think that what has been

00:26:55.380 --> 00:27:00.360
the nicest thing about this is my GSP
network has become his GSP network, and

00:27:00.360 --> 00:27:03.100
we get kind of double the network
which is really nice, so.

00:27:03.100 --> 00:27:04.540
&gt;&gt; I'm going to give
a slightly different take.

00:27:04.540 --> 00:27:05.970
I also have a significant other.

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:10.809
He's long distance,
lives in Chicago, my fiance,

00:27:10.809 --> 00:27:14.080
and so, I will say it's hard.

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:16.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's definitely hard.

00:27:16.580 --> 00:27:17.310
&gt;&gt; It's hard.

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:21.370
Like you go from maybe living together,
spending a lot of your time together.

00:27:21.370 --> 00:27:24.420
Even if you're working really hard to
being thrown into a completely different

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:28.060
environment where a lot of your
time is spent meeting new people,

00:27:28.060 --> 00:27:30.370
doing new GSB things and it's really.

00:27:30.370 --> 00:27:37.350
It can be really hard to integrate both of
you to live in that world and I just want

00:27:37.350 --> 00:27:41.480
to acknowledge it's hard because I think
it would be hard at any business school.

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:44.570
I'm actually sure it is
just given the experience.

00:27:44.570 --> 00:27:48.500
But just be aware of that and
kind of talk about it and

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:52.610
talk about how you're
going to work with that.

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:54.690
It's just a difference in lifestyle,

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:58.370
a difference in the time that you
can allocate to your relationship.

00:28:00.340 --> 00:28:02.270
It also, I will say something.

00:28:04.060 --> 00:28:07.640
In some ways it's been easier
because he's long distance.

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:10.160
So he doesn't have to hang
out with GSPers all the time,

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:11.230
he has his own group of friends.

00:28:11.230 --> 00:28:15.320
And in some ways it's harder, because at
this point, Val's husband has friends

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:17.720
inside and outside of the class,
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:19.860
&gt;&gt; Whom he also loves,
who've become part of his family,

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:21.350
and that happens around second year.

00:28:21.350 --> 00:28:24.780
So Anschel never quite got there, right?

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:29.720
But he never had the roughness of
me not being at home all the time,

00:28:29.720 --> 00:28:31.820
cause we were just apart.

00:28:31.820 --> 00:28:34.160
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I mean it's definitely a challenge,

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:36.510
you have to spend a lot of time thinking
about how you allocate your time.

00:28:36.510 --> 00:28:39.590
I think the one thing you'll
realize at business school is

00:28:39.590 --> 00:28:41.960
that time is the precious resource.

00:28:41.960 --> 00:28:46.507
And so when you have a relationship,
you need to figure out how you fit that in

00:28:46.507 --> 00:28:49.367
with all the other stuff
that you have going on.

00:28:49.367 --> 00:28:56.260
But lots of people are doing it,
there's lots of people talking about it.

00:28:56.260 --> 00:28:57.040
&gt;&gt; We also write it.

00:28:57.040 --> 00:28:58.144
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Even.

00:28:58.144 --> 00:29:00.890
&gt;&gt; Yes so.
Unfortunately we're almost out of time and

00:29:00.890 --> 00:29:05.090
we still have many questions but
just to summarize this, [INAUDIBLE] and

00:29:05.090 --> 00:29:09.010
Val what would be your final pieces
of advice before you graduate,

00:29:09.010 --> 00:29:11.040
what advice do you have?

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:12.960
For people watching today and,
I guess, for

00:29:12.960 --> 00:29:14.978
me too, since I'm going to
be a second year next year.

00:29:14.978 --> 00:29:18.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:29:18.220 --> 00:29:19.890
&gt;&gt; I think the one thing

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:23.160
that I've gotten out of the GSB, and I
would say it starts with the application,

00:29:23.160 --> 00:29:28.030
is this idea of authenticity,
which I find my dean talks about.

00:29:28.030 --> 00:29:31.580
But, the GSB focuses a lot on being
introspective and being really true to who

00:29:31.580 --> 00:29:37.209
you are and aligning your both
professional and personal values and

00:29:37.209 --> 00:29:41.030
desires to that.

00:29:41.030 --> 00:29:44.490
And it starts with the application,
what matters most to you?

00:29:44.490 --> 00:29:46.600
Really think about that question.

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:50.580
I did a lot of introspective research when
I did my essay and that really helped me

00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:55.840
figure out what I really wanted out of the
GSB, out of life, out of my personal life.

00:29:55.840 --> 00:30:01.250
And help me pave a part forward,
and I would say do the same, for

00:30:01.250 --> 00:30:04.350
you to do the same, because that journey
will just continue when you're here.

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:10.410
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think, I would echo
a lot of what Ritchie said, and

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:14.190
in addition, what made me choose
the GSB was the community,

00:30:14.190 --> 00:30:17.560
I think this is the best place
in the world, it's the most.

00:30:18.750 --> 00:30:21.770
&gt;&gt; Fantastic group of people and
supportive group of people.

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:26.380
In your application, and when you get
here, remember that you are an important

00:30:26.380 --> 00:30:29.680
part of that community because of
the unique things that you bring.

00:30:29.680 --> 00:30:34.850
I think I spent the first you
know quarter or half year even.

00:30:34.850 --> 00:30:37.110
Worrying that I wasn't
business school enough or

00:30:37.110 --> 00:30:40.880
that my background in government
wasn't valuable enough.

00:30:40.880 --> 00:30:45.160
And as I'm leaving and reflecting I'm
realizing that I had so much to offer.

00:30:45.160 --> 00:30:48.300
I have so much to offer that my
classmates have a lot to learn from me.

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:49.240
And I have a lot to learn from them.

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:52.290
And so I would say come in
with the confidence and

00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:57.360
apply with the confidence that you will
bring so much to the community yourself.

00:30:57.360 --> 00:31:01.090
And also remember that you have so much
to learn from the people that are here.

00:31:01.090 --> 00:31:03.860
And I think that when you can
embrace that give and take,

00:31:03.860 --> 00:31:07.960
you'll be in a place to really capitalize
on all that the GSB has to offer.

00:31:07.960 --> 00:31:09.580
I agree with that.

00:31:09.580 --> 00:31:11.450
&gt;&gt; I echo everything she and Bao said.

00:31:11.450 --> 00:31:15.254
I'll just add something that I heard
from someone, which blew my mind.

00:31:15.254 --> 00:31:19.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So applying to the GSB, most people see

00:31:19.460 --> 00:31:23.500
it as a marketing exercise, so marketing
themselves and trying to showcase all

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:26.840
the great things you guys have done,
which I'm sure are great things.

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:29.060
But people see it more as marketing.

00:31:29.060 --> 00:31:31.300
But rather you should
see it as accounting.

00:31:31.300 --> 00:31:33.030
Where you're trying to
take stock of yourself.

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:35.420
So you're trying to take
stock of your strengths and

00:31:35.420 --> 00:31:36.970
areas that you want to improve on.

00:31:36.970 --> 00:31:39.280
So yeah you're trying to
display those strengths but

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:42.450
you also want to be aware of what you
want to get out of the experience

00:31:42.450 --> 00:31:46.110
like those parts of your life that you
want to change or you want to improve.

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:48.511
So even though you want
to get into the GSB,

00:31:48.511 --> 00:31:53.070
you want to make sure it's a good
match both for you and for the GSB.

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:54.770
And just like Risha and Val said,

00:31:54.770 --> 00:31:58.680
you want to come through authentic in
your essay and in your application.

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:01.930
Just be yourself,
let your personality shine and

00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:04.820
just so we want to get to know you.

00:32:04.820 --> 00:32:05.420
&gt;&gt; Do I count in?

00:32:05.420 --> 00:32:05.925
Yeah.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:32:05.925 --> 00:32:07.060
&gt;&gt; Do I count in am I kidding?

00:32:07.060 --> 00:32:08.480
&gt;&gt; Okay, yeah.

00:32:08.480 --> 00:32:11.330
&gt;&gt; So thank you both for joining us today
&gt;&gt; Of course.

00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:12.980
&gt;&gt; You too.
&gt;&gt; Sharing all this wisdom.

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:14.012
&gt;&gt; Thank you for joining us.

00:32:14.012 --> 00:32:16.067
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thank you very much for

00:32:16.067 --> 00:32:16.657
joining us.

00:32:16.657 --> 00:32:19.300
You can learn more about the MBA
program on the GSP website,

00:32:19.300 --> 00:32:23.990
and the application for
the class after for

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:27.240
the class that's in the fall of
2017 will be available in June.

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:30.070
And if you have any questions
about the MBA program,

00:32:30.070 --> 00:32:33.170
you can submit them in the chat books
as you've been submitting them.

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:36.370
And the staff from
the MBA admissions office

00:32:36.370 --> 00:32:39.420
will be able to answer your question
within the next 15 minutes.

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:40.140
Thank you very much.

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:42.110
Again and have a wonderful day.

00:32:43.840 --> 00:32:44.450
Bye.
&gt;&gt; Bye.

